{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Verifier paths resolve the standard class\n\n## Context / Problem\nThree separate code paths pick the verifier model, and all three break off the default provider:\n- `review_kernel/verify.py:269` — `verifier_default if model == default_model else (model or\n  verifier_default)`. Any non-default model (every Bedrock id) reads as an explicit operator choice,\n  so the downgrade silently stops applying.\n- `completion.py:332` — `if cfg.model == DEFAULT_MODEL: cfg = replace(cfg, model=...)`. Same inversion,\n  separate path.\n- `workflow/gates/code-review.yaml` — declares no `model:` on any step, so all four code-review passes\n  take `cfg.model` and it never downgrades at all. Its Pass-2 pins `temperature: 0` for greedy\n  verification, so running it on the frontier model also loses determinism.\n\n## Approach\nReplace all three with a single resolution of the `standard` class. The equality test DISAPPEARS\nrather than being repaired: \"the operator chose a verifier\" is now expressed by configuring the\n`standard` slot, not by comparing `cfg.model` to a constant. One mechanism fixes three gates.\n\n**Alternative rejected — fixing the equality rule in place.** It would need a new way to distinguish\n\"operator override\" from \"provider change\" when both arrive as `cfg.model`, and it would have to be\nrepeated on all three paths.\n\n## Scope\n- `src/rebar/llm/model_classes.py` — add `resolve_model_string(value)`: the ONE place that knows the\n  reserved class vocabulary. Returns the resolved id for `trivial`/`standard`/`frontier`, and any\n  other string UNCHANGED.\n- `src/rebar/llm/config.py` — `resolve_model` calls it via a LAZY, function-level import. A\n  module-level import is IMPOSSIBLE here: `model_classes` imports from `config`, and its docstring\n  forbids the reverse. Function-level is the established pattern in this file already\n  (`config.py:108`, `:566`, `:768`).\n- `src/rebar/llm/review_kernel/verify.py` — `resolve_verifier_model`.\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/__init__.py` — `_verifier_cfg` (line 46). A consumer the plan had NOT\n  enumerated, with two further call sites at `xcheck.py:198` and `xcheck.py:336`.\n- `src/rebar/llm/completion.py` — the parallel equality test at line 332.\n- `src/rebar/llm/workflow/gates/code-review.yaml` — express the per-pass class as `model: standard`.\n- `tests/unit/` — per-path resolution tests.\n\nNOT in scope, and deliberately: the ladder path\n(`production_batch_runner.py:93`, `code_review/batch_runner.py:55`) is sibling task 7761. Both\ntickets call the SAME `resolve_model_string`, which is why the vocabulary lives in one function.\n\n## Testing\nContract tier: the observable is which model id each pass resolves to under a given class config.\n- each of the FOUR verifier paths resolves the `standard` class: `resolve_verifier_model`,\n  `_verifier_cfg`, `completion.py`'s equality test, and code-review.yaml's verify step.\n- with classes configured for a non-default provider, none of them falls back to the framework\n  default (the regression this ticket exists for).\n- code review's Pass-2 resolves `standard`, not `frontier`.\n- a literal model id passed to `resolve_model_string` comes back UNCHANGED (the back-compat\n  guarantee that keeps every existing workflow YAML working).\n\n## MECHANISM (decided on parent eb58 — corrected; do not re-derive)\n\n**A workflow step names a class by using the class name as its `model:` value.** The three class\nnames are RESERVED VALUES in the EXISTING model-string space — no new `class:` key, no schema\nchange. In YAML: `model: standard`.\n\n**There is NO single interception point.** An earlier version of this plan claimed\n`config.resolve_model` was \"the ONE funnel every step model passes through\". That was wrong three\nways, all verified in source:\n\n1. `config.py` cannot import `model_classes` at module level — the dependency runs the other way and\n   `model_classes`'s own docstring forbids the reverse.\n2. `model_ladder` never reaches `resolve_model`: `production_batch_runner.py:93` does\n   `dataclasses.replace(cfg, model=req.model_ladder[0])`.\n3. The verifier paths never reach it either — `resolve_verifier_model` is its own path, as is\n   `_verifier_cfg`.\n\nSo the shape is ONE FUNCTION, several CALL SITES:\n\n    model_classes.resolve_model_string(value: str) -> str\n        \"trivial\" | \"standard\" | \"frontier\"  ->  resolve via load_class_slots()\n        anything else                        ->  return unchanged\n\nThis ticket wires the VERIFIER call sites; sibling 7761 wires the LADDER ones. Keeping the\nvocabulary in one function is what stops those two tickets from drifting apart.\n\nREJECTED — a new `class:` key: forks a step schema `runs.py` documents as closed, and makes\n`model: standard` vs `class: standard` a silent ambiguity.\n\nREJECTED — resolving at YAML load time: the mapping depends on process-scoped env/CLI layers, so it\nwould freeze the wrong value in a long-lived process and be invisible to `provider_provenance`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] a test asserts plan-review Pass-2/Pass-4 resolve the `standard` class\n- [ ] a test asserts the completion verifier resolves the `standard` class\n- [ ] a test asserts code-review's verify step resolves the `standard` class rather than `cfg.model`\n- [ ] a test asserts that with classes configured for a non-default provider, no verifier path resolves\n      a model belonging to a different provider\n- [ ] `grep -rn \"== DEFAULT_MODEL\" src/rebar/llm/` returns no verifier-selection site\n\n- [ ] a test asserts a workflow step whose `model:` is the literal string `standard` resolves to the\n      configured standard class's model id, and that a step whose `model:` is a literal model id\n      resolves to that id UNCHANGED (the byte-for-byte back-compat guarantee)\n- [ ] a test asserts the Pass-2/Pass-4 verifier resolves the `standard` class REGARDLESS of what\n      `cfg.model` is — including when `cfg.model` is a Bedrock id. MEASURED today:\n      `resolve_verifier_model` downgrades ONLY on an exact match against the bare `claude-opus-4-8`,\n      so even `anthropic:claude-opus-4-8` (the SAME model, merely provider-qualified) silently keeps\n      Pass-2 on opus. This criterion fails against the current implementation, so it cannot pass\n      vacuously\n- [ ] a test enumerates the consumers of `resolve_verifier_model` and asserts each resolves the\n      standard class — `plan_review/__init__.py:_verifier_cfg` is one the plan had not enumerated\n- [ ] a test asserts `resolve_model_string` returns a literal model id UNCHANGED, so existing\n      workflow YAML keeps resolving byte-for-byte\n- [ ] a test asserts `_verifier_cfg` resolves the standard class, exercised through its real callers\n      at `xcheck.py:198` and `xcheck.py:336` rather than in isolation\n"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785501020884098001,"uuid":"58b87e1e-d1fc-4ced-920b-ef9624c4a550"}